  "Question": "Let \\(c\\) be a constant. The simultaneous equations \\[ \\begin{aligned} x-y&=2,\\\\ cx+y&=3. \\end{aligned} \\] have a solution \\((x,y)\\) inside Quadrant I if and only if",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(c=-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(c\\gt-1\\)",
    "C": "\\(c\\lt\\frac32\\)",
    "D": "\\(0\\lt c\\lt\\frac32\\)",
    "E": "\\(-1\\lt c\\lt\\frac32\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Inside Quadrant I, both coordinates must be positive. Adding the equations eliminates \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(c+1)x=5.\n\\]\nIf \\(c=-1\\), this is impossible. Otherwise,\n\\[\nx=\\frac{5}{c+1},\\qquad y=x-2.\n\\]\nSince the numerator of \\(x\\) is positive, \\(x>0\\) requires \\(c+1>0\\), or \\(c>-1\\). With that denominator positive, the remaining condition becomes\n\\[\ny>0\n\\iff \\frac{5}{c+1}>2\n\\iff 5>2(c+1)\n\\iff c<\\frac32.\n\\]\nConversely, every \\(c\\) satisfying both bounds gives \\(x>2\\) and \\(y=x-2>0\\). These values satisfy the first equation, and\n\\[\ncx+y=(c+1)x-2=5-2=3,\n\\]\nso they also satisfy the second.\n\nAt \\(c=-1\\) there is no solution; at \\(c=3/2\\), the solution is \\((2,0)\\), on an axis. Therefore the required condition is \\(\\boxed{-1<c<\\frac32}\\), choice E.",
